EFG 2 R "Kohlberg and Mertens (1986), Figure 2 (x = 2)" { "I" "II" }
"The game :math:`\Gamma(x)`, showing that conformity with backwards induction is necessary but not sufficient for strategic stability. Player I chooses T, which ends the game, M or B; player II, who is not told whether I chose M or B, chooses L or R.

The equilibrium (T,R) with payoffs (2,2) is sequential, but strategically unstable: B is strictly dominated by T, so if II has to move he should infer that I played M, hence play L, and I should then deviate to M; the unique strategically stable equilibrium is (M,L) with payoffs (3,3). For x > 1, B is also strictly dominated by M, and for x = 2 player I has the additional, more direct reason to deviate from T that T is dominated by M. The Nash equilibria form two connected components: the singleton (M,L) with payoffs (3,3), and the interval from (T,R) to (T,(0,1)), which is (T,R) itself, so that the second component is the single point (T,R), with payoffs (2,2).

The parameter x is 2 in this file, the upper end of the range :math:`1 < x \le 2` that the paper uses in its main discussion (the paper allows any :math:`x \le 2`)."

p "" 1 1 "" { "T" "M" "B" } 0
t "" 1 "" { 2, 2 }
p "" 2 1 "" { "L" "R" } 0
t "" 2 "" { 3, 3 }
t "" 3 "" { 2, 0 }
